1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,409
You charge in, put everything into it,
and the ball still floats down.

2
00:00:04,759 --> 00:00:09,303
In one study, club bowlers averaged
about 90 kilometres an hour.

3
00:00:09,653 --> 00:00:15,946
Australian first-class bowlers averaged
a top speed of about 127.

4
00:00:15,946 --> 00:00:20,714
Elite fast bowlers usually sit
around 130 to 145.

5
00:00:20,714 --> 00:00:25,482
And the very quickest have been
clocked above 160.

6
00:00:25,833 --> 00:00:27,599
That gap isn't just talent.

7
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:29,617
It's a ladder with five levels.

8
00:00:29,617 --> 00:00:33,022
Today: where you are, and the
drills that move you up.

9
00:00:33,722 --> 00:00:35,229
This is how I group it.

10
00:00:35,229 --> 00:00:38,117
It's a coaching ladder, not
an official scale.

11
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:40,410
Level 1, the Arm Slinger.

12
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:42,503
Level 2, the Rhythm Runner.

13
00:00:42,503 --> 00:00:44,671
Level 3, the Aligned Bowler.

14
00:00:44,671 --> 00:00:46,091
Level 4, the Whip.

15
00:00:46,091 --> 00:00:49,006
And Level 5, the Durable Strike Bowler.

16
00:00:49,355 --> 00:00:53,494
Each level builds on the one before, so
it pays to climb in order.

17
00:00:53,844 --> 00:00:58,175
The Arm Slinger walks in and tries to
bowl fast with the arm alone.

18
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:04,070
Across 18 studies, faster bowlers tended to
have quicker run-ups and stronger,

19
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:05,544
more powerful bodies.

20
00:01:05,893 --> 00:01:08,845
It's like throwing a dart with
just your wrist.

21
00:01:08,845 --> 00:01:11,674
You've left your legs and
body out of the job.

22
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:16,751
Fast bowling is one movement in phases:
run-up, gather, back foot lands,

23
00:01:16,751 --> 00:01:19,470
front foot lands, release, follow-through.

24
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:21,547
And you choose one shape.

25
00:01:21,547 --> 00:01:25,066
Side-on, with your back foot
parallel to the crease.

26
00:01:25,066 --> 00:01:27,722
Or front-on, with your chest more open.

27
00:01:27,722 --> 00:01:28,652
Both are fine.

28
00:01:29,002 --> 00:01:33,294
Before any bowling, running or
jumping drill in this video,

29
00:01:33,294 --> 00:01:36,870
warm up for 5 to 10 minutes:
easy jogging, skips,

30
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:39,946
walking lunges and a few
easy run-throughs.

31
00:01:40,296 --> 00:01:42,004
Drill 1: walk-throughs.

32
00:01:42,004 --> 00:01:45,491
3 walking steps into your
action at half effort.

33
00:01:45,491 --> 00:01:49,192
3 sets of 6 balls at a target,
filmed on your phone.

34
00:01:49,542 --> 00:01:51,614
Drill 2: stand and deliver.

35
00:01:51,614 --> 00:01:54,870
From the back-foot position,
step and bowl.

36
00:01:54,870 --> 00:01:57,311
3 sets of 6 at 60 to 70 percent.

37
00:01:57,311 --> 00:02:01,233
Front foot lands first, then the
arm comes over late.

38
00:02:01,583 --> 00:02:02,445
The mistake?

39
00:02:02,445 --> 00:02:04,036
Muscling the arm harder.

40
00:02:04,386 --> 00:02:07,821
The speed lives in the run-up, the
brace and the sequence.

41
00:02:08,521 --> 00:02:10,149
Level 2 is the run-up.

42
00:02:10,149 --> 00:02:14,964
Run-up speed is one of the most
consistent predictors of ball speed.

43
00:02:15,313 --> 00:02:18,340
The fastest part should be
the last 5 metres.

44
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:23,078
In one review, speed over that final
stretch tracked ball speed closely.

45
00:02:23,429 --> 00:02:25,269
Think of a motorway on-ramp.

46
00:02:25,269 --> 00:02:30,473
Build speed smoothly, and arrive at the crease
at the fastest speed you can still

47
00:02:30,473 --> 00:02:30,981
control.

48
00:02:31,331 --> 00:02:33,480
Faster isn't always better, though.

49
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:36,942
Your run-up has to match the action
you can hold together.

50
00:02:37,291 --> 00:02:39,203
Drill 1: mark your run-up.

51
00:02:39,203 --> 00:02:42,530
Start with 8 to 12 strides,
put down a marker,

52
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:45,504
and do 6 to 8 run-throughs without a ball.

53
00:02:45,855 --> 00:02:47,931
Drill 2: build-up runs.

54
00:02:47,931 --> 00:02:53,466
4 to 6 runs of 20 to 25 metres, with
the last 5 metres fastest.

55
00:02:53,466 --> 00:02:55,455
Walk back between each.

56
00:02:55,805 --> 00:02:59,763
Drill 3: run-up plus delivery
at 70 to 80 percent.

57
00:02:59,763 --> 00:03:04,808
2 to 3 sets of 6 balls, and count every
one in your weekly total.

58
00:03:05,157 --> 00:03:05,994
The mistake?

59
00:03:05,994 --> 00:03:10,758
A run-up so long or so fast that you
stutter and brake before the crease,

60
00:03:10,758 --> 00:03:12,560
throwing away your momentum.

61
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:17,923
Level 3 is the most important level, because
this is where you protect your back.

62
00:03:18,273 --> 00:03:23,065
A mixed action means your hips point one way
while your shoulders twist round the

63
00:03:23,065 --> 00:03:23,649
other way.

64
00:03:23,999 --> 00:03:28,782
Picture wringing out a wet towel, then
slamming it down on your front foot.

65
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:30,292
Your spine is the towel.

66
00:03:30,643 --> 00:03:35,486
That twist is linked to lower back pain and to
stress fractures in the low back.

67
00:03:35,835 --> 00:03:39,116
And in one small study of
high-performance bowlers,

68
00:03:39,116 --> 00:03:41,766
more twist didn't go with more ball speed.

69
00:03:42,116 --> 00:03:45,124
So the twist seems to buy you
risk, not pace.

70
00:03:45,924 --> 00:03:47,222
Then the front leg.

71
00:03:47,222 --> 00:03:51,311
In studies of men, the fastest
bowlers tend to land on a firm,

72
00:03:51,311 --> 00:03:52,804
straighter front knee.

73
00:03:52,804 --> 00:03:55,140
In women, the picture is more mixed.

74
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:57,720
It's like a pole vaulter's plant.

75
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,065
The leg stops, and the body is
catapulted forward.

76
00:04:01,065 --> 00:04:03,426
A collapsing knee leaks that energy.

77
00:04:03,776 --> 00:04:05,809
But that leg takes a big hit.

78
00:04:05,809 --> 00:04:10,688
The front foot absorbs roughly 5 to 9
times your body weight on landing.

79
00:04:11,038 --> 00:04:13,112
So you earn the brace with strength.

80
00:04:13,112 --> 00:04:14,793
You don't just lock your knee.

81
00:04:15,143 --> 00:04:16,978
Drill 1: the phone check.

82
00:04:16,978 --> 00:04:19,660
Film 6 balls from behind and side-on.

83
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:25,447
At back-foot contact, do your feet, hips and
shoulders point roughly the same way?

84
00:04:25,797 --> 00:04:29,626
If you see a big twist, get a
qualified coach to look.

85
00:04:29,626 --> 00:04:32,133
Changing an action is a coach's job.

86
00:04:32,483 --> 00:04:34,230
Drill 2: step and brace.

87
00:04:34,230 --> 00:04:39,959
From 3 steps, land on the front foot, firm the
knee, let your chest come over it,

88
00:04:39,959 --> 00:04:41,357
and hold 2 seconds.

89
00:04:41,357 --> 00:04:44,920
3 sets of 6, twice a week,
without a ball at first.

90
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:47,535
Drill 3: front-leg strength.

91
00:04:47,535 --> 00:04:50,971
Split squats with a slow
3-second lowering.

92
00:04:50,971 --> 00:04:54,486
2 to 3 sets of 8 to 12 per
leg, twice a week.

93
00:04:54,836 --> 00:04:55,719
The mistake?

94
00:04:55,719 --> 00:04:59,117
Trying to fix your action alone
from video clips.

95
00:04:59,117 --> 00:05:01,428
The twist is hard to judge by eye.

96
00:05:01,778 --> 00:05:04,978
At Level 4, power flows
through the whole chain.

97
00:05:05,328 --> 00:05:09,772
The front leg plants, the hips open, the
chest drives forward and down,

98
00:05:09,772 --> 00:05:11,685
and the arm comes through late.

99
00:05:12,035 --> 00:05:13,799
It's like cracking a whip.

100
00:05:13,799 --> 00:05:17,980
The handle stops sharply, and the
energy races down to the tip.

101
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:19,352
Your hand is the tip.

102
00:05:19,703 --> 00:05:21,187
One detail matters.

103
00:05:21,187 --> 00:05:27,051
Faster bowlers open up between hips and
shoulders late, at front-foot contact,

104
00:05:27,051 --> 00:05:28,684
not by twisting early.

105
00:05:29,034 --> 00:05:31,573
So the cue is "late", not "more twist".

106
00:05:32,273 --> 00:05:34,512
And don't lean sideways for pace.

107
00:05:34,512 --> 00:05:36,817
Side-bend is linked to back injury.

108
00:05:37,167 --> 00:05:41,612
Drill 1: plyometrics, the
best-supported speed method so far.

109
00:05:41,612 --> 00:05:44,695
In a review of small fast-bowling studies,

110
00:05:44,695 --> 00:05:48,495
jump training had a moderate
effect on bowling speed.

111
00:05:48,845 --> 00:05:53,147
Start low: about 60 easy foot
contacts, like pogo hops,

112
00:05:53,147 --> 00:05:55,759
twice a week with 2 days between.

113
00:05:55,759 --> 00:06:01,752
Over a few weeks, add bounds and skater
jumps, and stay at or under about 100.

114
00:06:02,101 --> 00:06:07,904
Land softly, on grass or a sprung floor, and
not the day before you bowl hard.

115
00:06:08,254 --> 00:06:10,729
Drill 2: a med-ball power circuit.

116
00:06:10,729 --> 00:06:14,547
In one small study, club bowlers
did med-ball throws,

117
00:06:14,547 --> 00:06:17,233
squats and split squats twice a week,

118
00:06:17,233 --> 00:06:20,627
plus bowling slightly heavier
and lighter balls.

119
00:06:20,977 --> 00:06:24,454
They gained about 5 kilometres
an hour in 4 weeks.

120
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:30,886
If you try modified balls, keep them within
about 10 to 15 percent of normal weight,

121
00:06:30,886 --> 00:06:34,392
use them with a coach, and count
them in your total.

122
00:06:34,742 --> 00:06:37,653
Under 18, or new to jumping and lifting?

123
00:06:37,653 --> 00:06:39,499
Get a coach to supervise.

124
00:06:39,499 --> 00:06:42,339
Had back pain or a recent growth spurt?

125
00:06:42,339 --> 00:06:43,617
Get checked first.

126
00:06:43,967 --> 00:06:44,850
The mistake?

127
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:47,228
Thinking the gym alone will do it.

128
00:06:47,228 --> 00:06:51,711
Lifting on its own showed only a small,
uncertain effect on speed.

129
00:06:52,061 --> 00:06:56,669
Level 5 is the bowler who's still fast in
the last over of the season.

130
00:06:57,019 --> 00:07:00,444
Fast bowlers are the most injured
players in cricket.

131
00:07:00,444 --> 00:07:04,314
Stress fractures in the low back
cost the most playing time,

132
00:07:04,314 --> 00:07:06,534
and they hit young bowlers hardest.

133
00:07:06,883 --> 00:07:08,782
The biggest trigger is the spike.

134
00:07:09,132 --> 00:07:14,298
In one study of elite bowlers, a week of
more than double their usual load,

135
00:07:14,298 --> 00:07:17,221
meaning their average over the last month,

136
00:07:17,221 --> 00:07:21,164
came with 3 to 4 times the injury
risk the following week.

137
00:07:21,514 --> 00:07:27,133
In English county pros, weeks of 234
balls or more, about 39 overs,

138
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:32,256
came with roughly 3 times the risk of
a back stress fracture,

139
00:07:32,256 --> 00:07:35,231
compared with weeks under about 200.

140
00:07:35,581 --> 00:07:39,648
Think of your body's load tolerance
like a savings account.

141
00:07:39,648 --> 00:07:42,021
Steady deposits build the balance.

142
00:07:42,021 --> 00:07:47,037
One huge withdrawal bounces, and the bill
can arrive a week or more later.

143
00:07:47,387 --> 00:07:50,091
But resting completely isn't safe either.

144
00:07:50,091 --> 00:07:54,854
Bowlers with too little regular bowling
were also more likely to get hurt.

145
00:07:55,204 --> 00:07:57,126
Drill 1: count your balls.

146
00:07:57,126 --> 00:07:58,906
Log every hard delivery.

147
00:07:58,906 --> 00:08:03,035
Build up gradually, never jump to
double your usual week,

148
00:08:03,035 --> 00:08:07,449
and borrow the junior rule: a lighter
week every 4 to 6 weeks.

149
00:08:07,799 --> 00:08:10,232
Drill 2: spread your bowling days.

150
00:08:10,232 --> 00:08:15,792
Elite bowlers had the lowest injury risk with
about 3 to 4 days between bowling

151
00:08:15,792 --> 00:08:16,418
sessions.

152
00:08:16,768 --> 00:08:22,036
So a good starting point for adults is 2
bowling days a week, well spaced,

153
00:08:22,036 --> 00:08:23,019
and 3 at most.

154
00:08:23,369 --> 00:08:24,157
Under 19?

155
00:08:24,157 --> 00:08:26,365
Follow your board's limits.

156
00:08:26,365 --> 00:08:33,066
Cricket Australia, for example, caps under-17s
at 5-over spells and 3 bowling days a

157
00:08:33,066 --> 00:08:33,460
week.

158
00:08:33,809 --> 00:08:35,413
Drill 3: back armour.

159
00:08:35,413 --> 00:08:40,517
An 8-week exercise programme made 14 to
17 year old bowlers stronger,

160
00:08:40,517 --> 00:08:46,715
with better back endurance, and slightly reduced
the twist without slowing the ball.

161
00:08:46,715 --> 00:08:48,465
It didn't test injuries.

162
00:08:48,815 --> 00:08:53,829
A good starting point: back holds, side
planks and single-leg squats,

163
00:08:53,829 --> 00:08:55,334
2 to 3 times a week.

164
00:08:55,334 --> 00:08:56,623
Keep it pain-free.

165
00:08:56,973 --> 00:09:01,484
Most important: if you get low back
pain while or after bowling,

166
00:09:01,484 --> 00:09:07,175
especially on one side, stop bowling and see a
sports doctor or physio before you

167
00:09:07,175 --> 00:09:07,938
bowl again.

168
00:09:08,289 --> 00:09:11,510
These bone injuries can start
before the pain does.

169
00:09:12,410 --> 00:09:13,484
A simple week.

170
00:09:13,484 --> 00:09:15,490
Monday: strength and jumps.

171
00:09:15,490 --> 00:09:18,211
Tuesday: rest, plus the back circuit.

172
00:09:18,211 --> 00:09:21,434
Wednesday: nets, run-up
rhythm, 4 to 6 overs.

173
00:09:21,784 --> 00:09:26,253
Thursday: lighter strength, a few jumps
and the back circuit again.

174
00:09:26,253 --> 00:09:27,173
Friday: rest.

175
00:09:27,173 --> 00:09:28,290
Saturday: match.

176
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:29,144
Sunday: rest.

177
00:09:29,494 --> 00:09:33,119
And before the season, build up
over 6 to 10 weeks.

178
00:09:33,119 --> 00:09:35,489
Don't go from zero to full spells.

179
00:09:35,839 --> 00:09:40,554
Run in with rhythm, line up, brace, whip it
through late, and count every ball.

180
00:09:40,903 --> 00:09:44,468
Climb one level at a time, and you
won't just bowl faster.

181
00:09:44,468 --> 00:09:47,730
You'll still be bowling fast at
the end of the season.
